   227	## Found while reporting the digests — the launcher's helper was bound by nothing
   228	
   229	Checking the closure membership for this section showed that `tools/exp11_pidrecord.py`
   230	entered `train`, `finalize`, `smoke` and `summarize_haa` (the trainer imports it) but
   231	**not** `launch_sh`: a shell has no imports for the closure walker to follow, so the
   232	helpers the launcher runs as subprocesses have to be listed in its spec, which is why
   233	`tools/exp11_lock_holder.py` is there. The module that now decides what every pid file in
   234	the arm means was in no approval key at all — its grammar could change under an approved
   235	`launch_sh` digest. RED `275be24^` asserts that every `tools/exp11_*` module named in the
   236	launcher's text is covered by some key, and that the two helpers with no key of their own
   237	are in `launch_sh`'s; GREEN `275be24` adds the file to the spec. That is what moved every
   238	exp_11 digest a second time, and the table above is the one that stands.
   239	
   240	## Notes for the reviewer
   241	
   242	* **The shell's reader is now a subprocess.** `pid_record` forks
   243	  `"$PYTHON" -m tools.exp11_pidrecord` per pid file, which is how the NUL problem is
   244	  closed for good: nothing but digits crosses back. Two consequences are worth stating.
   245	  (i) The launcher `cd`s to the repo root and exports `PYTHONPATH="$PWD"` at the top, so
   246	  the module always resolves; in a checkout where it did not, every other Python step of
   247	  the launcher would fail too. (ii) A reader that cannot run reads as "not a record",
   248	  which is the safe direction for `registration_complete` (the marker stays) and the
   249	  unsafe one for `pid_alive` (an attempt would read as not-live) — bounded now by the
   250	  marker rule, which keeps an arm closed until the trainer's own `train.exit` is there.
   251	  Cost is a few interpreter starts per scan (three pid files per attempt), on a path that
   252	  runs a handful of times per launch.
   253	* **`clear_launching` and a `--no-save` diagnostic.** A `--no-save` trainer writes no
   254	  sidecars by design, so a full-mode launch of one would leave its marker standing. Full
   255	  mode never passes `--no-save` (the flag belongs to the smoke path, which runs under
   256	  `diagnostic` and has no marker), so this is not reachable today; it is stated here
   257	  rather than guarded, because a guard would have to guess at a future caller.
   258	* The grace in `resolve_unregistered` is unchanged, and, as the review says, it now only
   259	  bounds how soon an operator may act: the tombstone is what makes a resolution final.
   260	
